~ tongue: bare
~ kernel/exceptions.glo — what to do when the CPU itself objects.
~
~ Vectors 0-31 are the CPU's own: it raises them when something is wrong with
~ the program that is running. We name the ones a young kernel meets most
~ often. Each handler tells the world and stops; the machine cannot go on,
~ because the instruction that faulted would simply fault again.
~ (`depart 99` ends QEMU with status 199 when a test device is plugged in,
~ and halts the machine for good when it is not.)

invoke "idt"

rite
  incant fatal_exception
    until 0
      call cli
      call hlt
    end
  end

  ward 0
    speak "exception: divide error"
    depart 99
  end

  ward 6
    speak "exception: invalid opcode"
    depart 99
  end

  ward 8
    speak "exception: double fault"
    depart 99
  end

  ward 13
    speak "exception: general protection fault"
    depart 99
  end

  ward 14
    speak "exception: page fault"
    depart 99
  end

  incant exceptions_init
    call idt_set with 0, (call ward_addr with 0)
    call idt_set with 6, (call ward_addr with 6)
    call idt_set with 8, (call ward_addr with 8)
    call idt_set with 13, (call ward_addr with 13)
    call idt_set with 14, (call ward_addr with 14)
  end
end
